1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,728
This is an original tabletop built from the visible Week 15 school
plan. It is not the school’s hidden assessment station, official
scoring sheet or Moodle quiz. Do not enter it as a result in the
school system.

2
00:00:13,728 --> 00:00:25,512
Have the current adult and pediatric job aids open. The timer is a
learning constraint, not a reason to skip safety, alter the algorithm
or invent a missing observation.

3
00:00:25,512 --> 00:00:39,480
For a small crew, functions may be combined as the incident plan
directs. The exercise lead announces who is coordinating each function
and where reports go; learners do not create a second command
structure.

4
00:00:39,480 --> 00:00:52,512
Rotate roles only at an explicit handoff. The next person repeats the
current hazards, patient count, triage totals, unit status,
destinations and open requests before accepting the role.

5
00:00:52,512 --> 00:01:07,608
A high-speed collision involves four vehicles on a two-lane highway.
Dispatch reports at least twelve people, including children. One
access road is partly blocked, the count is still an estimate and two
occupants have not been located.

6
00:01:07,608 --> 00:01:24,816
Your team has one ambulance at the scene. A second unit is delayed by
traffic. The scenario is designed to test the system, not to reward a
fast diagnosis: announce the capacity problem, request appropriate
resources and use the current incident structure.

7
00:01:24,816 --> 00:02:08,472
In this fictional rehearsal you are the assigned exercise incident
lead. Practice report: Dispatch, Unit twenty-one. Major incident
declared at Highway eight and Cedar Road: a four-vehicle high-speed
collision. At least twelve patients reported, including children; two
occupants remain unlocated. Individual conditions are not yet
confirmed. One access lane is partly blocked; no additional hazard has
been confirmed. Request additional ambulances and fire-service traffic
and extrication support through dispatch. Unit twenty-one will update
after the first count. This exercise role does not grant real
incident-command authority.

8
00:02:08,472 --> 00:02:22,776
The location and unit number are fictional training details. In a live
response, use the actual location, observed hazards and dispatched
resources. Never repeat the practice script as a real report.

9
00:02:22,776 --> 00:02:46,320
Use the current adult chart for each fictional card. Cards B through F
cannot walk on command. A is Minor; B is Deceased after airway
positioning fails to restore breathing; C, D and E are Immediate at
their first matching branch. F is Delayed because the earlier
breathing and perfusion branches are negative and the command is
followed.

10
00:02:46,320 --> 00:03:03,576
A tag is a rapid priority message, not a definitive diagnosis. In
practice, use the exact local labels, required patient identifier and
movement instructions. If the card leaves a key finding out, ask for
clarification rather than guessing.

11
00:03:03,576 --> 00:03:18,384
The posted BCEHS START aids set a target of 30 seconds per patient.
Keep the chart visible until the sequence is reliable. The aim is a
consistent first pass, not guessing faster or compressing a necessary
branch.

12
00:03:18,384 --> 00:03:33,408
The school may observe time, category, tag and communication
separately. Record each honestly. Finishing inside the target does not
prove accuracy, and a correct tag does not excuse an unsafe approach.

13
00:03:33,408 --> 00:03:47,520
After the first six cards, the scribe reports the count and the triage
leader confirms it against visible tags. The two unlocated occupants
remain “not yet found,” not silently dropped from the incident
picture.

14
00:03:47,520 --> 00:04:01,680
When a patient moves or is reassigned, update the incident’s approved
tracking method. If the digital system is unavailable, use the
fallback specified by command rather than creating a separate
undocumented process.

15
00:04:01,680 --> 00:04:16,968
A bus is found down an embankment, and six children are brought to the
safe sorting area. Use JumpSTART as assigned by the exercise lead.
Keep walking ability, respiratory branch, pulse and AVPU in their
correct order.

16
00:04:16,968 --> 00:04:30,120
This additional group changes patient count, staff needs and the
urgency of the next radio update. The triage lead informs command; the
team does not begin an uncoordinated transport surge.

17
00:04:30,120 --> 00:04:58,368
Cards B through D are non-walking; C and D are breathing
spontaneously. A enters the Minor walking branch. B remains apneic
after airway positioning but has a pulse; continued apnea after the
chart’s five breaths leads to Deceased on this tool. C is Immediate
because the breathing rate is above forty-five. D is Delayed after the
relevant breathing, pulse and appropriate pain-response findings.

18
00:04:58,368 --> 00:05:13,656
Do not use the adult respiratory threshold, a full neurological scale
or an age rule recalled from a textbook. If the child’s communication
or developmental needs make the tool uncertain, request the
appropriate secondary review through command.

19
00:05:13,656 --> 00:05:30,720
The second update should not repeat the original report word for word.
State that a bus has been found, six additional children are accounted
for, two vehicle occupants remain unlocated, access is still partly
restricted and the second ambulance is delayed.

20
00:05:30,720 --> 00:05:42,720
Ask command to confirm the next resource or traffic action and report
whether transport destinations can accept the current mix. Mark
estimates clearly and set the next update trigger.

21
00:05:42,720 --> 00:06:00,144
In the next run, a child previously sorted as Delayed has a reliable
respiratory rate of 48 per minute. Do not preserve the earlier
category because it is already on the tag. Reassess using JumpSTART
and update the priority according to the current chart.

22
00:06:00,144 --> 00:06:13,416
The update belongs to the incident system: tell the triage lead,
receiving treatment function and transport coordinator as the plan
requires. Keep the earlier value and the new value distinguishable in
the record.

23
00:06:13,416 --> 00:06:28,992
The transport lead does not simply choose the nearest facility. The
role coordinates the plan, unit availability, route and receiving
capacity; dispatch and command receive updates so vehicles do not
stack at one access point.

24
00:06:28,992 --> 00:06:41,712
Use the local patient identifier and authorized transport log. A head
count alone is not enough to reconnect the triage category, the
patient and the destination after the scene changes.

25
00:06:41,712 --> 00:06:58,320
The school asks learners to rotate command in the Sunday exercise.
Make the rotation an observable handoff rather than a silent change:
the incoming leader receives the current picture and accepts it, then
command identifies the new contact to the crews.

26
00:06:58,320 --> 00:07:08,304
If the handoff is interrupted, pause the role change and ask for the
missing part. Do not assume the new lead heard a critical hazard or
transport constraint.

27
00:07:08,304 --> 00:07:22,560
Begin with the timeline and observed actions. Ask what the team
expected, what actually happened, which update changed the plan and
whether the right role received it. Keep the discussion specific
enough that another observer could verify it.

28
00:07:22,560 --> 00:07:38,592
Look for system conditions as well as individual decisions: blocked
access, noisy radio traffic, duplicate tasking, missing tags,
transport delays or unclear role transfer. End with one change and a
repeated scenario to test it.

29
00:07:38,592 --> 00:07:53,688
A trainee may feel distress after assigning different priorities to
patients who all need help. Invite a concise reflection on the
decision and the resource constraint; do not ask the learner to prove
that they cared by disclosing personal details.

30
00:07:53,688 --> 00:08:08,232
A fair process uses the agreed tool consistently, communicates
uncertainty and asks for review when a case does not fit. The category
is a temporary operational message, not a judgment of worth or a
guarantee of outcome.

31
00:08:08,232 --> 00:08:24,144
Extended incidents require role coverage, relief, hydration, food and
rest to be planned by the appropriate command and logistics functions.
Do not invent fixed shift durations or rotations; use the actual local
incident plan.

32
00:08:24,144 --> 00:08:42,888
A strong emotional response can occur even when a team followed the
plan. Support should be respectful and voluntary. If distress
continues or interferes with sleep, safety or daily functioning, use
the available professional support route rather than relying on a
class debrief alone.

33
00:08:42,888 --> 00:08:57,504
This original checklist supports rehearsal. It does not assign an
official pass mark, replace the instructor’s current scoring form or
establish clinical competence. Keep any final performance record in
the school’s required format.

34
00:08:57,504 --> 00:09:10,440
After the run, give one observed strength, one specific gap and one
repeatable next action. Then rerun the changed segment so the learner
can demonstrate the repair rather than only describe it.
